<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Entitlements for the hardened-runtime, Developer ID-signed binary (#211).

    Two TCC surfaces, handled differently:

    1. Full Disk Access (kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAllFiles) — the SQLite read
       path. FDA has NO requestable entitlement key (there is no
       "personal-information.full-disk-access"); it is granted purely by the
       user in System Settings. So FDA needs nothing here.

    2. Apple Events / Automation — this server controls Mail.app via
       NSAppleScript (compose / send / move / rules, plus the AppleScript read
       fallback). Under a hardened runtime, a process may NOT send Apple events
       unless it carries com.apple.security.automation.apple-events. Without it,
       the Automation TCC prompt never appears and every AppleScript call fails
       with errAEEventNotPermitted (-1743) — i.e. a hardened-runtime build with
       NO entitlements would be WORSE than the old ad-hoc build. Caught by the
       #211 verify Codex cross-model lens (CODEX-1) after five Claude reviewers
       all missed it.

    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events is an unrestricted entitlement —
    valid for Developer ID distribution outside the App Store, no provisioning
    profile, no extra notarization requirements.

    Do NOT remove the apple-events key. EntitlementsPlistTests pins it; dropping
    it silently re-breaks all Mail control under a signed build.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
